---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import FeatureList from '../components/FeatureList.astro';
import WriteGate from '../components/WriteGate.astro';
import BadNotZero from '../components/BadNotZero.astro';
import { TIERS } from '../lib/tiers';
import { CURRENT, GATEWAY_FEATURES, product } from '../lib/products';

const protocols = (await getCollection('protocols')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const native = protocols.filter((p) => p.data.verification !== 'gateway-mode');
const releases = (await getCollection('releases'))
  .filter((r) => r.data.product === 'gateway')
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.getTime() - a.data.date.getTime() || b.data.version.localeCompare(a.data.version, undefined, { numeric: true }));
const latest = releases.find((r) => r.data.published)!;
const gw = product('gateway');
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop Gateway: OPC UA, Modbus and MQTT gateway"
  description="An industrial automation gateway with seven native protocols, ISA-18.2 alarms, a historian, and one write gate that nothing can route around."
  ogAlt="HotLoop Gateway, an industrial automation gateway."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: 'HotLoop Gateway',
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/gateway/',
      description:
        'An industrial automation gateway that polls equipment over OPC UA, Modbus, MQTT and Sparkplug B, EtherNet/IP, S7comm, MTConnect, and HTTP, keeps the history, raises ISA-18.2 alarms, and exposes the plant to agents over MCP.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      softwareVersion: CURRENT,
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop Gateway</span><span class="chip ok">{latest.data.version} is out</span></div>
      <h1>An industrial gateway that tells you what it has not proven.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        It reads real equipment over real protocols, keeps every reading with its quality, alarms on it, automates
        against it, and lets agents read the whole plant over MCP. What makes it worth running: it won't lie to you
        about a sensor it can't see, and it won't move a machine nobody said it could.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>{latest.data.version} is out, and it's the first published release since 4.3.1.</strong>
          <p>
            Every version from 4.4.0 to 4.15.3 got merged and never tagged, so they all ship at once, in this one.
            The image is <code>{gw.image}:{latest.data.version}</code>, the chart is <code>{gw.chart}</code> from
            {' '}<code>https://hotloop.io/hotloop</code>, and both pull with no login. The source repo is still private.
            On 4.3.1? Don't just <code>helm upgrade</code>. The chart changed its name, Kubernetes won't rename a
            selector in place, and you want to find that out from <a href="#upgrading">the upgrade notes</a>, not
            from a stuck rollout.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install it</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#the-gate">Try to break the write gate</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/releases/gateway/">Release notes</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Where it sits</span>
        <h2>The top of the lineup. Everything, for the whole site.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          HotLoop is one codebase shipped as four products, all from the same tag.
          {' '}<a href="/iot/">IoT</a> is the automation base, <a href="/edge/">Edge</a> adds the machine layer, and
          the Gateway is both of those plus what a whole site needs: fleet management, multi-site roll-ups and
          scheduled reports. The <a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a> is the headless one that polls and forwards.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          Right now the Gateway and the Edge Relay are the two you can install. IoT and Edge come in an upcoming
          release, and nearly everything they'll carry already runs in the Gateway. So if you need one box running a
          site today, this is the box.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">What only the Gateway has</span>
        <FeatureList features={GATEWAY_FEATURES} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Install</span>
        <h2>Helm, from the published chart.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Any Kubernetes, k3s included. The chart brings its own TimescaleDB, generates the database password, the
          MCP token and an admin account, and keeps all three across upgrades, so an upgrade never logs you out of
          your own plant.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2 split">
        <div class="stack">
<pre><code>helm repo add hotloop https://hotloop.io/hotloop
helm repo update
helm install hotloop hotloop/hotloop --version {latest.data.version} \
  --namespace hotloop --create-namespace</code></pre>
          <p class="muted">
            The user is <code>admin</code>. The chart generates the password, keeps it in the release Secret, and
            the install notes print the command that reads it back:
          </p>
<pre><code>kubectl -n hotloop get secret hotloop \
  -o jsonpath='&#123;.data.admin-password&#125;' | base64 -d
kubectl -n hotloop port-forward svc/hotloop 8080:8080</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="muted">
            Open <code>http://localhost:8080</code>, sign in, add a device, discover its tags. Then notice that you
            can't write to anything. That's on purpose. Every tag starts read-only and gets armed one at a time, and
            {' '}<code>safety.allowWrites</code> stays off until you decide this pod gets to move machinery. Nobody's
            first day with HotLoop should end with a press doing something they didn't ask for.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            The image is <code>{gw.image}:{latest.data.version}</code>, amd64 and arm64, about 34 MB, and it pulls
            anonymously. The database is TimescaleDB, or plain PostgreSQL when the extension isn't there. SQLite is
            coming, in the same image.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            There's no Quadlet unit for the Gateway yet, so off a cluster it's the chart on k3s. The
            {' '}<a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a> has one today. The
            {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/gateway/install/">install docs</a> cover the values worth setting
            before a real plant sees it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card upgrade reveal" id="upgrading">
        <span class="label">Coming from 4.3.1</span>
        <h3>It is not a plain helm upgrade.</h3>
        <p>
          Nothing between 4.3.1 and {latest.data.version} was ever published, so you're taking every version from
          4.4.0 to 4.15.3 in one jump, under a new name. The image was <code>ghcr.io/embernet-ai/industrial-iot</code>,
          the chart was <code>industrial-iot</code>, and every <code>IIOT_</code> variable is <code>HOTLOOP_</code> now.
          The chart name is baked into the workload selectors, and Kubernetes flat out refuses to change a selector
          in place. Keep the release name and the namespace, and:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><code>pg_dump</code> the database. Belt and braces, and you'll be glad you did.</li>
          <li>Delete the old Deployment (<code>&lt;release&gt;</code>) and StatefulSet (<code>&lt;release&gt;-postgresql</code>). The database volume is its own claim and stays put.</li>
          <li><code>helm upgrade &lt;release&gt; hotloop/hotloop -f your-4.3.1-values.yaml</code>, with any <code>IIOT_</code> in <code>extraEnv</code> renamed and any <code>image.repository</code> override dropped. Not <code>--reuse-values</code>, which skips every default the chart added since.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The new database pod mounts the old volume and the Gateway migrates it forward on start. We proved that by
          rendering both charts side by side, not by running it on a live cluster. That gap is exactly why step one
          exists.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three more things will surprise a script. There's a login now. Writes default to off. And the API changed
          in a handful of places, like empty lists coming back as <code>[]</code> and never <code>null</code>. The
          license is the HotLoop Community License now, not Apache-2.0. The full list and the procedure are in
          {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/gateway/upgrading/">the upgrade docs</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="the-gate">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">The write gate</span>
        <h2>Every write goes through one function, and nothing routes around it.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The UI, the API, a rule, a script, an agent: same function, same checks, same order. Policy lives in one
          place because the day it lives in five, somebody forgets the fifth, and that somebody's write goes to a
          live press.
        </p>
      </div>
      <WriteGate />
      <p class="prose muted after">
        An agent gets no side door. <code>write_tag</code> and <code>call_service</code> hit the same gate an operator
        does, and neither one runs without a stated reason, which lands in the audit next to the write. An audit row
        nobody can explain six months later is just a timestamp with a guilty conscience.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Bad is not zero</span>
        <h2>A failed read is not a value.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Every reading carries good, uncertain or bad. A driver that can't read a point still reports, marked bad,
          because silence looks exactly like a value that hasn't changed. Alarms hold their state on a bad reading
          instead of evaluating it, so a dead instrument shows up as a dead instrument and not as a process upset.
        </p>
      </div>
      <BadNotZero />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Built for agents</span>
        <h2>MCP in both directions.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          16 tools and 4 resources in {latest.data.version}, 25 tools on main, over streamable HTTP and stdio. An
          agent reads the plant in entities, the infeed pump rather than register 40001, and it can only act through
          the gate. So you can ask it why line 3 is slow and let it read everything, knowing it can only touch what
          you armed, with its reason on file.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          It goes the other way too: a rule can call a tool on another MCP server.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Many sites</span>
        <h2>One question, every plant.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Point one Gateway at the others and the Sites screen asks each of them the question it would answer
          itself. Each site keeps its own database, so a dead WAN link takes out a roll-up, never a plant.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          A site that doesn't answer shows as unreachable, with the reason, and stays out of the totals. Counting a
          plant you can't reach as zero alarms is how a head office decides everything is fine on the worst night
          of the year.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What it speaks</span>
        <h2>Seven native protocols, and a table that admits how far each has been proven.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-4">
        {native.map((p) => (
          <a class="card proto reveal" href={`/integrations/${p.id}/`}>
            <div class="cluster head">
              <h3>{p.data.name}</h3>
              <span class={`chip ${TIERS[p.data.verification].chip}`}>{TIERS[p.data.verification].label}</span>
            </div>
            <p>{p.data.summary}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="notice after">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>S7comm and EtherNet/IP have never talked to a physical PLC.</strong>
          <p>
            Their decoding is tested hard, and tested decoding is still not a value that came off a real controller.
            If you're the first to point either one at real hardware, treat it as commissioning: check a value you
            already know before you trust a single screen.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What's inside</span>
        <h2>The rest of the plant, in one binary.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          No sidecar, no second service to keep alive, no runtime to babysit. One static Go binary running as a
          non-root user with every capability dropped.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Historian</h3><p>TimescaleDB when the extension's there, native partitioning when it isn't. Per-tag deadband, so a sensor twitching in its last bit doesn't write a row every scan until the disk fills.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Alarms</h3><p>ISA-18.2, <code>rtn-unack</code> included. Acknowledged and cleared are separate, so the trip at 03:00 that fixed itself is still on the list when the morning shift walks in.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Automations</h3><p>Ten trigger types, entity state included, and conditions compiled on save. A broken rule gets caught in the editor, not at 3am halfway through a batch.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The Board</h3><p>The screen that lives on a wall. Gauges with the alarm limits drawn where they fall, and a layout stored with the plant, so the panel PC and your laptop always show the same thing.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Entities</h3><p>The infeed pump, not register 40001. Every tag gets an entity for free, and a motor, valve or PID loop is one entity built from the tags it really is. Rules, screens and agents read like the plant, not like a register map.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Plant tree</h3><p>One ISA-95 tree, enterprise down to equipment, plus labels. Filter a whole area in one click, and the site, area and line your devices already carry stay in step with it.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Paging</h3><p>ntfy, Gotify, Discord, webhook and email. Press Acknowledge on your phone and the alarm is acknowledged in the Gateway, signed and single use. Discord has only met a stand-in, not the real thing.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Reports</h3><p>Historian numbers on a schedule, as CSV or HTML, stored and emailed. Only good readings count, every row says how many it threw out, and an empty period says no data, never zero.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>OPC UA server</h3><p>Read-only, secured, and Ignition 8.3.9 reads it end to end. The SCADA you already paid for can see every tag, and there is no write handler in there to worry about.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Edge Relay</h3><p>Its own product with its own image and chart: about 15 MB against the Gateway's 34, no database, no UI. The Gateway's fleet view tracks and commands every relay. <a href="/edge-relay/">More on the Edge Relay</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Embedded broker</h3><p>An optional MQTT broker in the same process, so a single box can be its own Sparkplug hub. No per-topic access control, by design. At scale, run a real broker.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Backups</h3><p>A snapshot in pure Go over the connection it already has. Restore loads an older backup by column name and flat refuses one from a newer version, so a restore can't half-work.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">On main since {latest.data.version}</span>
        <h2>Merged, tested, and landing in the next release.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          None of this is in {latest.data.version}. It merged after the tag and ships in the next release. Until then
          you can't install it, and this page isn't going to pretend otherwise.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Recipes</h3><p>Grade A's zone temps, line speed and die gap, off the laminated sheet and into the plant in one go. Checked whole and refused whole, written in order, read back from the device. Nobody fat-fingers the changeover at 2am again.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Scripts</h3><p>Write the CIP cycle once, name it, and run it the same way from a screen, a rule, MCP or its own entity. A dry run goes through the real gate, so you find out what it would refuse before anything moves.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Blueprints</h3><p>Write the rule once with blanks, fill it in per pump. A rule keeps the blueprint version it was made from, so editing the blueprint on Wednesday doesn't quietly change the rule that was right on Tuesday.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Helpers</h3><p>Toggles, numbers, selects, text, counters, timers and schedules: the values the plant's people own, like today's batch target or which shift is on. Kept across restarts, never clamped.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Logbook</h3><p>State changes, writes, alarms, rule runs and config changes on one timeline, for the plant, one place on the tree, or one entity. "What happened right before?" becomes one list instead of five screens.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The automation language</h3><p>Condition, wait and stop steps inside a sequence, and <code>forSec</code> on a state trigger. "The pump has been on for five minutes" is one trigger now, not a timer hack.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="prose muted after">
        Also on main: the read-only half of a native <a href="/unifi/">UniFi integration</a>. Its first real
        read called a dead 5G backup link up, because it trusted the same stale field the console did. It reads the
        console's health checks now, so a backup that's been dead for two days reads dead.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free. For you, and for your business through EmberNET.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            The Gateway is source-available under the HotLoop Community License, which is not an OSI open source
            license. There are no license keys and nothing unlocks at runtime. Individual, home, hobbyist, nonprofit
            and educational use is free. Any business or for-profit use goes through
            {' '}<a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNET</a>, where signing up is free and running HotLoop is free.
            Support, SLAs and warranties come from our Official Systems Integrators,
            {' '}<a href="https://fireballz.ai">Fireball Industries</a>, who'll also design, install and support the
            rollout if you'd rather not.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the terms</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .page-hero .notice code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .upgrade { margin-top: var(--hl-s7); display: grid; gap: var(--hl-s3); }
  .upgrade ol { padding-left: 1.3em; display: grid; gap: var(--hl-s2); color: var(--hl-ink-soft); font-size: var(--hl-fs-sm); }
  pre { max-width: 100%; }
  .after { margin-top: var(--hl-s6); }

  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .proto { text-decoration: none; color: inherit; display: block; transition: transform var(--hl-transition-fast), border-color var(--hl-transition-fast); }
  .proto:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); border-color: var(--hl-orange); }
  .proto .head { justify-content: space-between; align-items: start; margin-bottom: var(--hl-s2); }
  .proto h3 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-md); margin: 0; }

  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
